<h1>Interface metric anomaly on cedge-60-07ae8615 (4 interfaces) — possible local link fault</h1>
<div class="badges">
  
  <span class="pill blue">Artifact class: Operational incident assessment</span>
  <span class="pill red">Incident: Active</span>
  <span class="pill gray">Recovery: Failed validation</span>
  <span class="pill amber">Fault domain: Suspected</span>
  <span class="pill blue">Root cause: Under investigation</span>
  <span class="pill amber">Possibly because of local link fault</span>
  <span class="pill gray">Impact: Not observable</span>
  <span class="pill amber">Ticket: Held</span>
  <span class="pill amber">Severity: HIGH</span>
</div>
<div class="meta">Report <b>rr-3e0400bcaa22-v3</b> · Case <b>P-3E0400</b> · Generated <b>2026-11-12 04:57:15 UTC</b> · The title names what was observed; the leading explanation is still missing required evidence</div>
<div class="note">Withheld for this artifact class (Operational incident assessment): lessons learned. unpromoted candidate — an operational assessment may exist while the incident is active; suspected/undetermined findings are allowed and lessons-learned editing is not</div>






<div class="pmh">1 · What broke</div>
<section>
  <p class="lead">Interface metric anomaly on cedge-60-07ae8615 (4 interfaces)</p>
  <h2>Incident at a glance</h2>
  <div class="kv">
    <span class="k">Where it happened</span><span class="v">Evidence concentrates on cedge-60-07ae8615; not independently localised.</span>
    <span class="k">Cause</span><span class="v">Possibly because of local link fault</span>
    <span class="k">Possible owner(s)</span><span class="v">NetOps</span>
  </div>
  <h2>Management summary</h2>
  <div class="mgmt">At 2026-11-12 04:50:50 UTC, automated telemetry detected a condition of “interface metric anomaly” affecting cedge-60-07ae8615. A recovery was observed on the affected component at 2026-11-12 04:54:36 UTC, but anomalies on the same component continued through 2026-11-12 04:54:41 UTC, so it is not recovered. The condition is ongoing (6m 24s elapsed). Customer impact could not be assessed — impact telemetry coverage was unavailable or insufficient for this window. The cause remains unconfirmed because independent evidence classes did not corroborate the observations. Decision: Hold — The evidence does not meet the ticket-open policy. Auto-ticketing holds until the policy threshold is met. Escalation follows the policy thresholds stated in the decision section.</div>
</section>


<div class="pmh">2 · Who is affected</div>
<section>
  <table><thead><tr><th style="width:26%">Who</th><th style="width:17%">Status</th><th>What we know</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr>
    <td><b>Real users</b></td>
    <td><span class="pill gray">Not measured</span></td>
    <td>No real-user telemetry covered this case. Not measured is not the same as not affected.</td>
  </tr><tr>
    <td><b>Synthetic checks</b></td>
    <td><span class="pill gray">Not measured</span></td>
    <td>No synthetic check covered this case.</td>
  </tr><tr>
    <td><b>Real traffic (flow records)</b></td>
    <td><span class="pill gray">Not measured</span></td>
    <td>No traffic-flow change is attached to this case.</td>
  </tr><tr>
    <td><b>Sites</b></td>
    <td><span class="pill gray">Not measured</span></td>
    <td>No site is named by the evidence.</td>
  </tr><tr>
    <td><b>Applications</b></td>
    <td><span class="pill gray">Not measured</span></td>
    <td>No application is named by the evidence.</td>
  </tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <div class="note">Impact is read only from impact measurements. A confirmed fault does not by itself mean anyone was affected, and a security finding is never counted as impact.</div>
</section>


<div class="pmh">3 · When</div>
<section>
  <div class="kv">
    <span class="k">First seen</span><span class="v">2026-11-12 04:50:50 UTC</span>
    <span class="k">Status</span><span class="v">Still active</span>
    <span class="k">Duration</span><span class="v">6m 24s so far (still active)</span>
    
    
  </div>
  
  <h2>Incident phases</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th style="width:22%">Phase</th><th style="width:30%">When</th><th>What happened</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr>
    <td><b>Detection</b></td>
    <td>2026-11-12 04:50:50 UTC</td>
    <td>First anomalous evidence observed.</td>
  </tr><tr>
    <td><b>Degradation</b></td>
    <td>2026-11-12 04:50:50 UTC → 2026-11-12 04:54:36 UTC</td>
    <td>Anomalous evidence persisted across the participating evidence classes.</td>
  </tr><tr>
    <td><b>Component recovery</b></td>
    <td>2026-11-12 04:54:36 UTC</td>
    <td>Component status recovery observed.</td>
  </tr><tr>
    <td><b>Residual degradation</b></td>
    <td>2026-11-12 04:54:36 UTC → 2026-11-12 04:54:41 UTC</td>
    <td>A recovery was observed on the component at 2026-11-12 04:54:36 UTC, but anomalies on the same component continued through 2026-11-12 04:54:41 UTC — it did not stay recovered.</td>
  </tr>
  </tbody></table>
  
  <div class="note">Only dated events are listed. A standing finding with no start time is in section 7, not here. The full timeline is in appendix B.</div>
</section>


<div class="pmh">4 · Cause and how sure we are</div>
<section>
  <div class="phrase suspected">Possibly because of local link fault</div>
  <p>Root cause has not been identified — possibly because of a local access link is faulty or flapping (unconfirmed best hypothesis).</p>
  
  
  <div class="kv">
    <span class="k">Possible cause (unconfirmed)</span><span class="v">A local access link is faulty or flapping.</span>
    <span class="k">Evidence in hand</span><span class="v" style="font-weight:400">Interface metric anomaly (4 observations)</span>
    <span class="k">Evidence still missing</span><span class="v" style="font-weight:400">Link up/down; Only device telemetry saw this — a second independent source is needed to confirm.; Only one source (cedge-60-07ae8615) reported this — a second, independent source is needed to confirm.; No trusted routing evidence in this window.</span>
  </div>
  
  
  <h2>Why it is not firmer</h2>
  <ol class="actions"><li>Only device telemetry saw this — a second independent source is needed to confirm.</li><li>Only one source (cedge-60-07ae8615) reported this — a second, independent source is needed to confirm.</li><li>No trusted routing evidence in this window.</li></ol>
  
  
  <h2>What would confirm it</h2>
  <ol class="actions"><li>Obtain an independent observation of the same fault from a second evidence class (routing/link state, traffic flow, device health, or an active check from an independent vantage).</li><li>Observe Link up/down.</li></ol>
  
  
</section>


<div class="pmh">5 · How we know</div>
<section>
  
  <ol class="actions"><li>The device&#39;s own counters showed interface metric anomaly (4 observations).</li></ol>
  
  
  <p><b>What the evidence shows:</b> Anomalies in device health, device alarms align in the same time window and scope. The device CPU/memory change and the device alarm are not evidence for the leading explanation (Local link fault), which needs interface metric anomaly and link up/down.</p>
  <p><b>Independent sources:</b> Suspected — sources agree, but no independent pair confirms the fault yet.</p>
  
  <div class="note">No causal sequence is proposed for this case — the engine has not matched a propagation pattern, and none is invented.</div>
  
</section>


<div class="pmh">6 · Where the break is</div>
<section>
  <p>Evidence concentrates on cedge-60-07ae8615; not independently localised.</p>
  
  
  <div class="note">The network path was not measured for this case, so no break is drawn.</div>
  
  
</section>


<div class="pmh bg">7 · Background on this device (not a cause)</div>
<section class="background">
  <div class="note">Nothing recorded on this device.</div>
</section>


<div class="pmh">8 · Other explanations considered</div>
<section>
  
  <table><thead><tr><th style="width:36%">Explanation</th><th>Why it is not the answer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td><b>Local DNS resolver failure</b></td><td>Not observed — none of the observations it requires was seen (it needs: Dns failure rate or Dns latency high; Synthetic http fail or Fqdn probe fail). Listed as the nearest competitor — not observed.</td></tr><tr><td><b>MAC address flapping</b></td><td>Not observed — none of the observations it requires was seen (it needs: MAC address flap). Listed as the nearest competitor — not observed.</td></tr><tr><td><b>OSPF adjacency flap</b></td><td>Not observed — none of the observations it requires was seen (it needs: OSPF neighbor change). Listed as the nearest competitor — not observed.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  
</section>


<div class="pmh">9 · Who owns the fix</div>
<section>
  <div class="kv">
    <span class="k">Owner</span><span class="v">NOC</span>
    <span class="k">Why</span><span class="v" style="font-weight:400">Default triage owner until the failure stage and fault domain are identified. Escalation owner is assigned when the analysis reaches probable/confirmed with a supported fault domain.</span>
    
  </div>
  
  <table style="margin-top:6px"><thead><tr><th>Team that may own it</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>NetOps</td><td>named by the &#34;Local link fault&#34; hypothesis (suspected)</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
</section>


<div class="pmh">10 · What to do now · Watch for recurrence</div>
<section>
  <h2>What to do now</h2>
  <ol class="actions">
  <li><span class="pill blue">P2</span> <b>Check interface counters (errors/discards/flap count) and the transceiver/cable on the affected port</b> — owner: NOC<div class="note">Expected output: interface admin/oper state, error and discard counters, and the active route toward the target</div></li>
  <li><span class="pill blue">P2</span> <b>Correlate the link-state syslog timestamp with the interface metric anomaly on the same interface</b> — owner: NOC<div class="note">Expected output: interface admin/oper state, error and discard counters, and the active route toward the target</div></li>
  <li><span class="pill blue">P2</span> <b>If the port is an uplink, verify the peer side and fail over to a redundant member if available</b> — owner: NOC<div class="note">Expected output: interface admin/oper state, error and discard counters, and the active route toward the target</div></li>
  <li><span class="pill red">P1</span> <b>Find out why the component did not stay recovered — a recovery was observed, but anomalies continued on the same component</b> — owner: NOC<div class="note">Expected output: the component&#39;s anomalies stop after the recovery, or the part that is still failing is isolated</div><div class="note">Escalate when: anomalies continue on the component after its recovery</div></li>
  <li><span class="pill blue">P2</span> <b>Continue monitoring under the active policy</b> — owner: NOC<div class="note">Expected output: auto-close after the stability window, reopen on recurrence</div><div class="note">Escalate when: customer impact is confirmed, or a second independent evidence class corroborates the fault</div></li>
  
  </ol>
  <h2>Decision</h2>
  <div class="decision ">
    <b>Hold</b> — The evidence does not meet the ticket-open policy. Auto-ticketing holds until the policy threshold is met.
  </div>
  <h2>Watch for recurrence</h2>
  <ul class="actions"><li>Monitoring window: 30m 00s after recovery.</li><li>Reopens when the same condition recurs within 30m 00s (flap suppression).</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="note">Durable corrective-action commitments are managed in the case's action register; a printed document is a status snapshot as of generation time.</div>
</section>


