<div class="divider">Appendix C · Glossary</div>
<section>
  
  <div style="margin:5px 0"><b>Observed</b> — Directly measured by an attached telemetry source inside this incident&#39;s window. An observed statement is a fact about what a source recorded — not an interpretation.</div>
  <div style="margin:5px 0"><b>Confirmed</b> — Established by independent, agreeing evidence sources — never by one source repeating, and never by timing alone.</div>
  <div style="margin:5px 0"><b>Suspected</b> — Supported by some evidence but not independently confirmed; alternative explanations remain open.</div>
  <div style="margin:5px 0"><b>Independent evidence</b> — Evidence from measurement systems with no shared failure mode (for example an active check, a routing-plane event and a traffic measurement). Their agreement is information; one system repeating is not.</div>
  <div style="margin:5px 0"><b>Real-user impact</b> — Effect on actual user traffic, shown only by evidence from real sessions or the serving infrastructure itself. It is a separate question from synthetic-check failures and is never derived from them.</div>
  <div style="margin:5px 0"><b>Not measured</b> — No telemetry with valid provenance quantifies this value for this case. Not measured is stated as such — it is never rendered as zero, and it is not evidence of no impact.</div>
  <div style="margin:5px 0"><b>Coverage gap</b> — An interval where a telemetry class produced no data. A gap is absence of measurement, not evidence of health — nothing is concluded from it.</div>
  <div style="margin:5px 0"><b>Nearest competitor</b> — An explanation the engine ranked although none of its required observations was seen — only optional context matched. It is listed so you can see what else was weighed; it is not observed and not a live candidate.</div>
  
  
</section>


